This paper systematically profiles how SFT-injected contamination affects benchmark scores across model families. Through controlled injection (LoRA, five dosage levels) into five models spanning four independent families from four labs (Alibaba, Microsoft, Allen AI, Google), we discover that contamination does not produce a single effect. Instead, five distinct dose-response patterns emerge: from immunity (Phi-4 14.7B, $\pm$0.5pp on MMLU) to catastrophic U-type collapse (Qwen-14B, $-$10pp). A baseline ability threshold at approximately 64\% MMLU separates models that benefit from contamination (OLMo-2 $+$3.2pp, Gemma-2 $+$3.8pp) from those that are harmed (Qwen-14B $-$10pp, Qwen-7B $-$3pp) or immune (Phi-4). The 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B, contradicting the assumption that scale confers robustness. We use ContamCal, a two-stage pipeline combining multi-signal item filtering with contamination-augmented IRT (2PL+$\gamma$), to provide the statistical machinery for this characterization: Stage~1 fuses detection signals via training-free Reciprocal Rank Fusion \citep{cormack_2009_rrf}; Stage~2 embeds exposure scores as covariates in an IRT model that jointly estimates ability $\theta$ and domain-level contamination effects $\gamma_d$.

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Five-pattern contamination taxonomy.} Controlled SFT injection across five models from four independent families reveals five dose-response patterns (Immune, U-collapse, V-recovery, Benefit, Benefit-saturation), governed by a baseline ability threshold at ${\sim}$64\% MMLU. These patterns are directionally consistent across MMLU and ARC-Challenge. Within the Qwen family, the 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B, directly contradicting scale-robustness assumptions. Within-family domain analysis reveals additional heterogeneity (Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, surviving Bonferroni correction).
    \item \textbf{Signal failure characterization and enabling analysis.} Min-K\%++ exhibits directional reversal at the 0.5B scale (Cohen's $d = -1.55$ to $-1.98$), where the signal is actively misleading. RRF-guided item filtering is a prerequisite for IRT convergence: single-signal selection causes convergence failure ($\hat{R} = 1.53$), while RRF fusion preserves broader distributions necessary for $\gamma$ identifiability ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random).
\end{enumerate}
Additionally, simulation studies establish that reliable $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$ at signal AUC $\geq 0.55$ (CI width $\propto J^{-0.48}$), above current practice ($J = 8$--$30$). Current results use single-seed data; triple-seed validation is in progress.","numLines":10,"startLine":5,"totalLines":15}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1a6bd81c-7616-42b1-af2f-556b816c5c73","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-cli","cwd":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/.claude/sessions/paper_agent_8415d3a6","sessionId":"04927545-9c64-4062-9fb4-11b15cebf913","version":"2.1.126","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
